-- BUSINESSES (179)

Columbus arrived with a fleet of 17 ships and more than a thousand settlers. After finding La Navidad — the fort left behind on his first voyage — destroyed, he chose this Atlantic shore, in Taíno land, to build a town with stone buildings: his own house, a church, a royal storehouse and dwellings. By tradition, the first official Mass in the New World was celebrated here on January 6, 1494.

Life in the settlement was hard: disease, food shortages and hurricanes led to its abandonment within a few years, and the colony''s center moved to Santo Domingo on the south coast.

Today the site is a national historical park. Twentieth-century archaeological excavations, including those led by José María Cruxent and Kathleen Deagan, uncovered the foundations of Columbus''s house, the church, the storehouse and a cemetery. The site has a small museum and paths among the seaside ruins.

Visiting: the site lies beside Villa Isabela in Puerto Plata province. Opening hours and entry fees are not listed here yet — please confirm them locally before you go.',ARRAY[]::text[],'','','','','','{}'::jsonb,'','',false,false,'listed','La Isabela fue fundada por Cristóbal Colón a finales de 1493, durante su segundo viaje a las Américas. Es reconocida como el primer asentamiento europeo permanente del continente americano. Llevó el nombre de la reina Isabel I de Castilla.

Colón llegó con una flota de 17 barcos y más de mil colonos. Tras encontrar destruido el fuerte de La Navidad, establecido en su primer viaje, eligió esta costa atlántica, en tierra de los taínos, para levantar una villa con edificaciones de piedra: su propia casa, una iglesia, un almacén real y viviendas. Según la tradición, el 6 de enero de 1494 se celebró aquí la primera misa oficial en el Nuevo Mundo.

La vida en la villa fue dura: enfermedades, escasez de alimentos y huracanes llevaron a abandonarla en pocos años, y el centro de la colonia se trasladó a Santo Domingo, en la costa sur.

Hoy el lugar es un parque nacional histórico. Excavaciones arqueológicas del siglo XX, entre ellas las de José María Cruxent y Kathleen Deagan, sacaron a la luz los cimientos de la casa de Colón, la iglesia, el almacén y un cementerio. El sitio cuenta con un pequeño museo y senderos entre las ruinas frente al mar.

Para visitar: el sitio está junto a Villa Isabela, en la provincia Puerto Plata. Horarios y precios de entrada aún no figuran aquí; confírmalos localmente antes de tu visita.','La Isabela was founded by Christopher Columbus in late 1493, during his second voyage to the Americas. It is recognized as the first permanent European settlement in the Americas. It was named after Queen Isabella I of Castile.

Columbus arrived with a fleet of 17 ships and more than a thousand settlers. After finding La Navidad — the fort left behind on his first voyage — destroyed, he chose this Atlantic shore, in Taíno land, to build a town with stone buildings: his own house, a church, a royal storehouse and dwellings. By tradition, the first official Mass in the New World was celebrated here on January 6, 1494.

Life in the settlement was hard: disease, food shortages and hurricanes led to its abandonment within a few years, and the colony''s center moved to Santo Domingo on the south coast.

Today the site is a national historical park. Twentieth-century archaeological excavations, including those led by José María Cruxent and Kathleen Deagan, uncovered the foundations of Columbus''s house, the church, the storehouse and a cemetery. The site has a small museum and paths among the seaside ruins.

Visiting: the site lies beside Villa Isabela in Puerto Plata province. Opening hours and entry fees are not listed here yet — please confirm them locally before you go.','','',null,null,null,null,'seed','TouristAttraction'),
